+
+## Synthetic production-volume query probe (codex/volume-indexes)
+
+`rebuild/scripts/volume-probe.ts` seeds synthetic production-like volumes in an
+already migrated, disposable local database. It does not start an app server,
+call providers, apply migrations or use customer fixtures. From `rebuild/`:
+
+```sh
+DATABASE_URL='postgresql://<user>:<password>@127.0.0.1:5432/volume_rehearsal' \
+  TSX_TSCONFIG_PATH=tsconfig.json node --import tsx scripts/volume-probe.ts \
+  > volume-probe-report.json
+```
+
+Use a dedicated rehearsal database with the reviewed root migration set already
+applied. The URL must explicitly use `localhost`, `127.0.0.1` or `[::1]`, have a
+simple database name ending in `_rehearsal`, and contain no query parameters or
+fragment. Production mode is refused. Before writing, the probe also checks the
+connected database name and PostgreSQL server address. There is no fallback to a
+preview, remote host or production URL. URL/driver errors are masked.
+
+At scale 1 the fixture contains 20 workspaces, 200 selected channels, one million
+Root events, 100,000 posts (including unconnected drafts), 200,000 conversations
+and messages, about 171,430 top-level comments, 20,000 reviews, one million channel
+insights and 100,000 account/campaign insight rows. Sparse literal search matches,
+multiple statuses, a historical date spread and unrelated workspace rows exercise
+filtering and joins. `VOLUME_PROBE_SCALE` accepts 0.01–10; use 0.01 for a smoke run,
+then 1 or a larger production-matched scale for planning evidence. The JSON
+records actual inserted row counts, scale, scenario wall time, emitted SQL,
+Prisma duration and `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS, FORMAT JSON)` plans. It never prints
+bound parameters or returned records. Missing measurements are unavailable, never
+zero. Output is emitted only after the fixture transaction has rolled back.
+
+The probe installs its transaction as the read-service Prisma singleton before
+importing the services. It calls current Root activity list/search/export, Posts
+first/deep page and search, Inbox merged activity first/continuation/search, and
+Analytics snapshot services. PostgreSQL plans replay the exact SQL and bindings
+captured from these service calls, including counts and relation hydration.
+Prepared-statement inference restores explicit PostgreSQL parameter casts for
+timestamps, enums and arrays lost in Prisma query-event JSON;
+provider-owned Analytics SQL remains in its adapter. Authorization, enabled
+modules and selected resources run normally against synthetic memberships and
+policy. Nonsecret registry defaults and synthetic feature/platform availability
+are installed inside the same transaction to make the scenario repeatable;
+existing policy and all fixture rows are rolled back on success or failure.
+No new Root keys are introduced. Run only against an idle disposable database:
+`ANALYZE` and seeding take locks/resources and aborted bulk inserts may leave
+space for vacuum to reclaim. The transaction has a 30-minute total timeout,
+120-second per-statement timeout and 5-second lock timeout.
+
+Review actual scans, rows removed, sort/spill nodes, buffer reads/hits and
+execution time together; a sequential scan alone does not justify an index.
+Compare before/after on identically seeded databases and record PostgreSQL
+version, hardware, existing indexes, scale and warm/cold-cache conditions.
+Only propose additive indexes backed by those plans, in one root migration
+ordered after the current latest `20261030110000_rebuild_ads_creative_creation`.
+The probe never installs candidate indexes automatically.
+
+Evidence for this batch: guard unit tests and the raw-SQL void-select guard pass;
+TypeScript checks pass. An additional full-suite run was interrupted after it
+stopped producing output without a final summary; it is not a full-suite pass.
+The CLI refuses a missing URL before importing Prisma.
+No rehearsal URL was configured in this worktree, so database seeding, plan
+replay, timings and rollback have **not** been exercised on PostgreSQL. No index
+migration is proposed without that evidence. Production-volume verification
+remains an open Root parity/release gate; synthetic plans, when collected, will
+not establish live-provider, production or concurrent-load performance.
